# Global fd ignore, read automatically from $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fd/ignore, with no
# flag or env var wiring it up. gitignore syntax.
#
# This exists for signal-to-noise, not for speed. fd walks a 19,462-file PHP
# project tree in ~60ms and rg searches it in ~340ms; --hidden costs no
# measurable time at all (0.342s vs 0.341s). The problem is what the walk
# returns: `$FD` in zsh/extra/fzf.zsh passes --hidden, and in ~/dotfiles that
# makes CTRL-T list 501 files of which 415 (83%) are .git/ internals. Object
# files and refs crowd out the 86 files you actually wanted to open.
#
# Excluding it here rather than adding --exclude to $FD fixes every fd caller at
# once: the CTRL-T and ALT-C pickers, _fzf_compgen_path / _fzf_compgen_dir
# behind `vim **<tab>`, and bare `fd` on the command line. One place, and the
# $FD string in fzf.zsh stays about color instead of growing exclusions.
#
# `fd -I` (--no-ignore) still bypasses this, so the escape hatch documented in
# .claude/CLAUDE.md keeps working when you genuinely need to search a .git
# object or a vendored tree.
.git/

# macOS sprinkles these through every directory it has ever previewed. Never the
# thing being searched for.
.DS_Store

# node_modules/, vendor/ and dist/ are deliberately not listed. Real repos
# already gitignore them, so fd skips them without help, and .claude/CLAUDE.md
# calls them out as trees you sometimes do need to search. A global exclusion
# would put them behind -I permanently and fight that workflow to buy nothing.
